    {%- when 'amethyst' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--amethyst">
        <p>Pricing & Origins</p>
        <p>Amethyst's value is shaped by the depth and evenness of its purple, clarity, and cut quality. It is one of the more accessible gemstones in the collection, but the range in quality is wide — and the difference between a well-selected stone and a pale, poorly cut one is significant both visually and in terms of astrological use.</p>
        <p>Brazilian Amethyst runs from soft lavender to deep violet — good clarity, widely available, and one of the most familiar sources globally. Price ranges from ₹600 to ₹10,000+ per carat.</p>
        <p>African Amethyst (Zambia, Madagascar, Namibia) tends toward deeper, richer purple with stronger saturation and a cleaner, more commanding look. Price ranges from ₹800 to ₹15,000+ per carat.</p>
        <p>For Saturn-related astrological use, clarity and colour stability matter more than origin alone. Choose the stone, not the label — a well-saturated Brazilian Amethyst will serve better than a pale African one, and vice versa. Colour should be rich and even, neither too pale to carry presence nor so dark it turns flat and muddy.</p>
        <p>Best metal: Silver and white metals are commonly preferred and suit the stone's aesthetic particularly well. Gold may also be used.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'citrine' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--citrine">
        <p>Pricing & Origins</p>
        <p>Citrine's value is shaped by the warmth and evenness of its colour, clarity, and cut quality. It is one of the most accessible stones in the collection, but quality varies meaningfully — and a well-chosen stone looks and feels categorically different from a pale, poorly cut one.</p>
        <p>African Citrine runs from lemon-yellow to golden tones with good clarity and consistent, value-driven quality. Price ranges from ₹500 to ₹3,000+ per carat.</p>
        <p>Brazilian Citrine tends toward honey, golden, and amber hues with excellent transparency and a more refined finish. It often commands a modest premium at equal quality. Price ranges from ₹500 to ₹5,000+ per carat.</p>
        <p>For astrological use, clarity, colour stability, and honest disclosure matter more than origin or size. A well-saturated African stone will serve better than a pale Brazilian one — choose the stone in front of you, not the label. Colour should feel warm and alive: lemon yellow through warm honey to gentle gold. Too pale and the stone lacks presence; brownish or muddy and it loses the brightness that makes Citrine what it is.</p>
        <p>Best metal: Silver, Gold, and Panchdhatu are all commonly used. Final choice may depend on design preference or astrological advice.</p>
      </div>
